{"version":"2.14","children":[{"type":"text","id":"cmp-title","content":"Three versions of the Matrix RL film, matched scene by scene","fontSize":40,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","x":886,"y":45606},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-head","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"children":[{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h0","content":"Neo Was the Agent (Original)\n22 scenes · 10m52s","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","width":400,"textGrowth":"fixed-width"},{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h1","content":"Learning Simulation v1\nphotoreal · 48 takes · 15:12","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","width":340,"textGrowth":"fixed-width"},{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h2","content":"Learning Simulation v2\nvector · 38 takes · 12:50","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","width":340,"textGrowth":"fixed-width"},{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h-v3","content":"Learning Simulation v3\ndraft · 21 scenes · 8:58","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","width":340,"textGrowth":"fixed-width"},{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h3","content":"Your notes","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#2f9e44","width":280,"textGrowth":"fixed-width"},{"type":"text","id":"cmp-h4","content":"New script\n20 frames · ~1,320 words · ~10 min","fontSize":26,"fontWeight":"700","fill":"#e8590c","width":420,"textGrowth":"fixed-width","x":0,"y":-16}],"x":886,"y":45672},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-flow","layout":"vertical","gap":90,"children":[{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-1","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s01-wake","width":400,"height":411,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s01-wake](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s01-wake.jpg)\n\n**01** · `0:00` · *Ch. 01: Wake up*\n\nImagine you wake up in a corridor you have never seen. Agents are closing in from both ends. Somewhere down the hall, behind one of ten identical doors, a telephone is ringing. That phone is your only way out."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-1","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s01-phone","width":340,"height":311,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s01-phone](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s01-phone.jpg)\n\n`s01-phone`\n\nListen. Somewhere out in this city, a telephone is ringing."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s02-reward","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s02-reward](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s02-reward.jpg)\n\n`s02-reward`\n\nIn reinforcement learning, that ringing phone is your terminal reward. It is the only exit. It is the only positive feedback in the entire simulation."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s03-city","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s03-city](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s03-city.jpg)\n\n`s03-city`\n\nNow look at what stands between you and that sound: ten miles of concrete and digital rain. Rooftop chasms, bricked-up traps, and armed agents waiting behind every turn. Every single hazard can delete you. None of them give you a score. Every intermediate step gives you a reward of exactly zero."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-1","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s01-listen","width":340,"height":311,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s01-listen](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s01-listen.jpg)\n\n`s01-listen`\n\nListen. Somewhere out in this city, a telephone is ringing."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s02-reward","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s02-reward](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s02-reward.jpg)\n\n`s02-reward`\n\nIn reinforcement learning, that ringing phone is your terminal reward. It is the only exit, and the only positive feedback in the entire simulation."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s03-corridors","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s03-corridors](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s03-corridors.jpg)\n\n`s03-corridors`\n\nBetween you and that sound lie miles of dark corridors, bricked-up traps, and hazards that can delete you in an instant."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-1","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s01-listen","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s01-listen](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s01-listen.jpg)\n\n`s01-listen` · 0:03\n\nListen. Somewhere in this maze, a telephone is ringing. That phone is your way out. In reinforcement learning, it is your reward, and the only one in the whole simulation. But between you and that sound lies a maze of rooms you have never seen: dead ends, traps, and Agents waiting around corners. You don't know how many steps it will take, or which of them will get you killed."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-1","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 01 (v3):\n\ngenerate a realistic phonebooth as in v2. Use the ringing phone as the first scene, ditch the title page. then animate and shrink the phone to the rop right corner of the grid (maybe use the v1 style swipe and show green wireframe technique). Also note that the phone icon you drew doesn't look like a phone, please fix","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-01","width":420,"height":350,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**01** · *The ringing telephone* · `new, gridworld opening`\n\nImagine you wake up in a corridor you have never seen. Somewhere in this city, a telephone is ringing. That phone is your only way out.\n\nBut between you and that sound lies a maze of rooms you have never seen: dead ends, traps, and Agents waiting around corners. You don't know how many steps it will take, or which of them will get you killed.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** big rain, camera moves and show the telephone, ringing, then it moves past the city, showing ladders, furnaces, agents, and finally Neo, standing in a hallway, doors ahead."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-2","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s02-credit","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s02-credit](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s02-credit.jpg)\n\n**02** · `0:27` · *Ch. 01: Wake up*\n\nYou have no map. Every second you hesitate, they get closer. And the reward, escape, only comes at the very end. So which first door do you open? This is the oldest puzzle in learning from experience. It is called the credit assignment problem: when the payoff arrives late, which of your choices earned it?"},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-2","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s04-doors","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s04-doors](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s04-doors.jpg)\n\n`s04-doors`\n\nCamera pulls all the way back to where you stand. You are trapped in an empty corridor facing fifty closed doors. You have no map. You have never walked these streets. How does an intelligence figure out which first door to open to reach a reward that is thousands of steps away?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s05-credit","width":340,"height":311,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s05-credit](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s05-credit.jpg)\n\n`s05-credit`\n\nThis is the credit assignment problem: the foundational riddle of reinforcement learning."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-2","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s04-which","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s04-which](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s04-which.jpg)\n\n`s04-which`\n\nHow does an intelligence figure out which first door to open to reach a reward thousands of steps away?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s05-credit","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s05-credit](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s05-credit.jpg)\n\n`s05-credit`\n\nWe are trying to learn a policy: a rulebook telling you which door to choose in every room. And our biggest challenge is credit assignment: discovering which early decisions actually deserve the credit."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-2","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s02-policy","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s02-policy](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s02-policy.jpg)\n\n`s02-policy` · 0:28\n\nSo how does an intelligence figure out which first door to open, when the reward is many steps away? What we are trying to learn is a policy: a rulebook that tells you which door to choose in every room, so that you collect as much total reward as possible. And our biggest challenge is credit assignment: discovering which early decisions actually deserve the credit."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-2","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 02 (v3):\n\nI like the v1's corridor animations, a lot of doors, the agent has to choose (leave out the messy trajectories)","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-02","width":420,"height":320,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**02** · *The ringing telephone* · `v2 narration, v1 visuals`\n\nHow does an intelligence figure out which first door to open, to reach a reward many steps away? They all try their best to learn a policy, a rulebook telling them which door to choose in every room, so that they walk out of this place alive.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** previous frame's neo turn into wireframe, v1's Neo model standing in a maze room with doors on several sides, not a straight corridor. The word POLICY stamps an arrow into every room; then faint backward arrows from the phone to early rooms, each with a ?."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-3","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s03-loop","width":400,"height":433,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s03-loop](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s03-loop.jpg)\n\n**03** · `0:53` · *Ch. 02: Follow the white rabbit*\n\nEvery learner, human or machine, lives inside one loop. You look at where you are: that is your state. You pick a move: an action. The world answers with a reward, or a penalty, and drops you somewhere new. State, action, reward, next state. Around and around."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-3","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s06-loop","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s06-loop](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s06-loop.jpg)\n\n`s06-loop`\n\nTo tackle the credit assignment problem, we first need a framework to describe what is happening. That framework is the reinforcement learning loop: state, action, reward, next state."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s07-loop2","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s07-loop2](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s07-loop2.jpg)\n\n`s07-loop2`\n\nYou observe where you stand: your state. You choose a move: an action. The world advances, delivers an outcome: a reward or penalty. And it drops you into a new state. State, action, reward, next state."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-3","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s06-loop","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s06-loop](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s06-loop.jpg)\n\n`s06-loop`\n\nTo tackle credit assignment, we need a simple framework to describe what an agent actually does. That framework is the reinforcement learning loop: state, action, reward, next state."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s07-loop2","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s07-loop2](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s07-loop2.jpg)\n\n`s07-loop2`\n\nYou observe the room where you stand: your state. You choose a door: an action. The world delivers an outcome: a reward or penalty. And you land in a new room: your next state."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-3","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s03-loop","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s03-loop](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s03-loop.jpg)\n\n`s03-loop` · 0:54\n\nTo describe what an agent actually does, we need one simple loop. You observe the room where you stand: your state. You choose a door: an action. The world delivers an outcome: a reward or penalty. And you land in a new room: your next state."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-3","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 03 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-03","width":420,"height":327,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**03** · *Follow the white rabbit* · `v2 s07, original's grid to v2's 3D`\n\nThese learners, human or machine, they all live inside one loop. You look at where you are: that is your state. You pick a move: an action. The world answers with a reward, or a penalty, and drops you somewhere new. State, action, reward, next state. Around and around.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Start on the original's flat grid map. As each word lands, the grid extrudes into v2's 3D wireframe maze: state lights Neo's room, action opens a door, reward pops a chip, next state moves the camera through."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-4","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s04-agent","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s04-agent](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s04-agent.jpg)\n\n**04** · `1:22` · *Ch. 02: Follow the white rabbit*\n\nNow here is the twist hiding in plain sight. In the film, the men in dark suits and sunglasses are called Agents. But in reinforcement learning, the word means the opposite. The agent is not the enforcer. The agent is the learner. Neo is the agent. The Matrix is his environment."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-4","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s08-teacher","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s08-teacher](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s08-teacher.jpg)\n\n`s08-teacher`\n\nIn this loop, there is no way to know which door is best beforehand. Even Morpheus refuses to be a supervised teacher. He gives Neo the foundational truth of reinforcement learning: \"I can only show you the door. You're the one that has to walk through it.\""},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s09-trial","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s09-trial](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s09-trial.jpg)\n\n`s09-trial`\n\nNo teacher stands behind you. No supervisor hands you labeled data or blueprints of the city. Because you cannot know the right answer in advance, you have to discover the best trajectory entirely through trial and error."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s10-policy","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s10-policy](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s10-policy.jpg)\n\n`s10-policy`\n\nYou take an action under uncertainty, observe whether you collect a reward or suffer a penalty, and update your policy: your rulebook for what to choose the next time you face this room. You explore unknown doors to discover secrets, exploit proven paths to stay alive, and turn raw consequences into intelligence."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-4","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s08-trial","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s08-trial](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s08-trial.jpg)\n\n`s08-trial`\n\nIn supervised learning, a teacher labels every move. In reinforcement learning, you get no labels. You discover the best policy entirely through trial and error: exploring unknown doors to find new paths, exploiting proven ones to survive, and turning raw consequences into intelligence."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-4","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s04-trial","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s04-trial](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s04-trial.jpg)\n\n`s04-trial` · 1:11\n\nIn supervised learning, a teacher labels every move. In reinforcement learning, you get no labels. You discover the best policy entirely through trial and error: exploring unknown doors to find new paths, exploiting proven ones to survive, and turning raw consequences into intelligence."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-4","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 04 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-04","width":420,"height":328,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**04** · *Follow the white rabbit* · `v2 s08, grid trajectories`\n\nIn supervised learning, a teacher labels every move. In reinforcement learning, you get no labels. You discover the best policy entirely through trial and error.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Pull back from the 3D maze to the 2D grid. Trajectories draw across it one after another: red lines ending in traps, a few green lines reaching the phone. Explored rooms brighten."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-5","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s05-oracle","width":400,"height":411,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s05-oracle](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s05-oracle.jpg)\n\n**05** · `1:48` · *Ch. 03: The Oracle*\n\nWhen Neo visits the Oracle, notice what she does not do. She never gives him directions. No turn left, no take the third door. Instead, she tells him something about where he stands."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-5","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s11-nomap","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s11-nomap](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s11-nomap.jpg)\n\n`s11-nomap`\n\nIf the hallways keep shifting, memorizing a step-by-step route is doomed to fail. But what if you didn't need a map at all?"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-5","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s09-generalize","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s09-generalize](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s09-generalize.jpg)\n\n`s09-generalize`\n\nWe don't want our learned policy to work in just one specific world; we want it to generalize. If you only memorize a fixed trajectory, one changed door breaks everything. So instead of memorizing paths, we learn what is the best state to be in."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-5","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"v3-none-5","width":340,"content":"*Dropped in v3.*","fill":"#e9ecef"}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-5","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 05 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-05","width":420,"fill":"#e9ecef","content":"**05** · ~~dropped~~\n\n(The Oracle scene is dropped.)"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-6","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s06-value","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s06-value](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s06-value.jpg)\n\n**06** · `2:08` · *Ch. 03: The Oracle*\n\nIn reinforcement learning, that verdict is called a value. The value of a place is all the reward you can expect to collect from here on, if you play well. Picture a glowing number painted on the floor of every room. Near zero: a trap. High: a step toward the exit. If you knew every number, you would never need a map. You would just keep stepping toward the brighter one."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-6","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s12-value","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s12-value](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s12-value.jpg)\n\n`s12-value`\n\nIn reinforcement learning, we give every state a score called a Value Function, or V(s). It asks a simple question: standing here right now, how much total reward can you expect to collect before the run ends?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s13-uphill","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s13-uphill](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s13-uphill.jpg)\n\n`s13-uphill`\n\nNow, you don't need to see the ringing telephone miles away. At every intersection, you just look down and step into whichever room has the higher number. A value function turns a complex maze into a simple walk uphill."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-6","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s10-value","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s10-value](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s10-value.jpg)\n\n`s10-value`\n\nIn reinforcement learning, we give every room a single score called the Value Function, written as V(s). It asks one fundamental question: standing here right now, how much total future reward can you expect to collect before the run ends?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s11-ninety","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s11-ninety](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s11-ninety.jpg)\n\n`s11-ninety`\n\nA room right next to the exit might be worth ninety points. A room heading into a dead end is worth only ten."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s12-uphill","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s12-uphill](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s12-uphill.jpg)\n\n`s12-uphill`\n\nOnce every room has a score, navigating is effortless. You don't need to see the telephone miles away. At every intersection, you simply look at the adjacent rooms and step into whichever has the higher number. A value function turns an impossible maze into a simple walk uphill."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-6","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s05-value","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s05-value](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s05-value.jpg)\n\n`s05-value` · 1:33\n\nA straightforward way to learn the best policy is to work out which room is the best to be in. So we give every room a single score, called the value function, or V for short. It asks one question: standing here right now, how much total future reward can you expect to collect before the run ends? A room right next to the exit might be worth ninety points. A room heading into a dead end is worth only ten."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s06-uphill","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s06-uphill](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s06-uphill.jpg)\n\n`s06-uphill` · 1:59\n\nOnce every room has a score, you simply step into whichever neighbour has the higher number. A value function turns an impossible maze into a simple walk uphill."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-6","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 06 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-06","width":420,"height":438,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**06** · *The value of a room* · `v2 narration tweaked, v1 visuals`\n\nA straightforward way to learn the best policy is to work out which room is the best to be in. So we give every room a single score called the value function, written V of s. It asks one question: standing here right now, how much total future reward can you expect to collect before the run ends? A room right next to the exit might be worth ninety points. A room heading into a dead end is worth only ten. Once every room has a score, you simply step into whichever neighbour has the higher number. A value function turns an impossible maze into a simple walk uphill.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v1's glowing numbers on the floor of each room, amber for value. 90 beside the exit, 10 in a dead end. Neo steps room to room toward the brighter number."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-7","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s07-bellman","width":400,"height":433,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s07-bellman](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s07-bellman.jpg)\n\n**07** · `2:37` · *Ch. 04: The Bellman equation*\n\nBut how do you work out a number that depends on your entire future? In the 1950s, the mathematician Richard Bellman noticed that you only have to look one step ahead. The value of where you stand is the reward you get here, plus the value of wherever you step next."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-7","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s14-bellman","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s14-bellman](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s14-bellman.jpg)\n\n`s14-bellman`\n\nHow do you calculate that number on the floor without simulating fifty steps of future chaos all the way to infinity? In 1953, Richard Bellman proved you only need to look one single step ahead."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s15-equation","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s15-equation](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s15-equation.jpg)\n\n`s15-equation`\n\nThe value of where you stand right now equals the immediate reward in this room, plus the discounted value of wherever you step next."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-7","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s13-fifty","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s13-fifty](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s13-fifty.jpg)\n\n`s13-fifty`\n\nHow do you calculate that score without simulating fifty steps of future chaos all the way to infinity?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s14-bellman","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s14-bellman](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s14-bellman.jpg)\n\n`s14-bellman`\n\nIn 1953, the mathematician Richard Bellman proved something remarkable: you only ever need to look one single step ahead."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s15-equation","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s15-equation](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s15-equation.jpg)\n\n`s15-equation`\n\nThe value of where you stand right now equals any immediate reward in this room, plus the discounted value of wherever you step next."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-7","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s07-bellman","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s07-bellman](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s07-bellman.jpg)\n\n`s07-bellman` · 2:14\n\nBut how do you work out a number that depends on your entire future? In the 1950s, a mathematician named Richard Bellman noticed something remarkable: you only ever have to look one step ahead. The value of where you stand is the reward you get here, plus the value of wherever you step next."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-7","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 07 (v3):\n\nCan you use a real history photo/clip of bellman?","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-07","width":420,"height":284,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**07** · *Look one step ahead* · `original`\n\nBut how do you work out a number that depends on your entire future? In the 1950s, the mathematician Richard Bellman noticed that you only have to look one step ahead. The value of where you stand is the reward you get here, plus the value of wherever you step next.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Original: Bellman portrait card, then the one-step look-ahead from Neo's room to its neighbour."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-8","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s08-discount","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s08-discount](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s08-discount.jpg)\n\n**08** · `3:02` · *Ch. 04: The Bellman equation*\n\nWith one adjustment: tomorrow's value is multiplied by a discount, say 0.9. Why? Because Agent Smith is getting closer. A phone ten rooms away is worth having, but every room you search costs time and risk. Ten rooms out, that reward is worth about a third of a reward right now. That is the Bellman equation: a promise about the future, checking itself one step at a time."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-8","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s16-discount","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s16-discount](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s16-discount.jpg)\n\n`s16-discount`\n\nWhy discounted? Why multiply tomorrow's promise by gamma, say 0.9? Because Agent Smith is closing in. A ringing telephone ten rooms away is wonderful, but every extra room you search costs time, risk, and decay. The discount rate gamma is the clock: a reward ten doors away is worth 0.9¹⁰, only about a third of a reward right now."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s17-promise","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s17-promise](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s17-promise.jpg)\n\n`s17-promise`\n\nBellman's equation is a promise checking itself: today's value must balance tomorrow's reality."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-8","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s16-discount","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s16-discount](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s16-discount.jpg)\n\n`s16-discount`\n\nWhy discounted? Why scale tomorrow's value by gamma, say 0.9? Because reward today beats reward tomorrow. Answering the phone right now pays one hundred points. Waiting ten doors down shrinks its value to 0.9¹⁰: about thirty-five points, a bit more than a third of the immediate reward."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-8","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s08-discount","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s08-discount](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s08-discount.jpg)\n\n`s08-discount` · 2:34\n\nWhy discounted? Why scale tomorrow's value by gamma, say 0.9? Because reward today beats reward tomorrow. Answering the phone right now pays one hundred points. Wait ten doors down, and its value shrinks to 0.9¹⁰: about thirty-five points, a bit more than a third of the immediate reward. That is the Bellman equation: a promise about the future, checking itself one step at a time."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-8","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 08 (v3):\n\nfix the phone icon here too","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-08","width":420,"height":350,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**08** · *Look one step ahead* · `v2, with original's equation`\n\nWhy discounted? Why scale tomorrow's value by gamma, say 0.9? Because reward today beats reward tomorrow. Answering the phone right now pays one hundred points. Waiting ten doors down shrinks its value to 0.9 to the tenth: about thirty-five points, a bit more than a third of the immediate reward. That is the Bellman equation: a promise about the future, checking itself one step at a time.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v2's visuals, with the original's V(here) = r + γ·V(next) equation building term by term; γ stamps on each of ten doors as 100 shrinks to 35."}],"x":0,"y":2},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-9","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s09-architect","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s09-architect](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s09-architect.jpg)\n\n**09** · `3:33` · *Ch. 05: The Architect*\n\nNow take the Architect's chair. You built the Matrix. You hold the Keymaker's keys: a complete map of which door leads where. In reinforcement learning, that map is called a model. With a model, you never have to walk the halls. You can compute every value from your chair, with an algorithm called value iteration."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-9","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s18-architect","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s18-architect](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s18-architect.jpg)\n\n`s18-architect`\n\nNow suppose you sit in the Architect's chair. You designed the Matrix. You hold the Keymaker's keyring: the complete environment model, transition dynamics P(s′ | s, a) and reward functions R."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s19-policydep","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s19-policydep](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s19-policydep.jpg)\n\n`s19-policydep`\n\nWhen you hold this omniscient blueprint, how do you actually calculate what every room is worth? Here is the fundamental rule: a room has no standalone value in a vacuum. Its evaluation is always subject to a chosen policy, written as Vπ(s)."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s20-behave","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s20-behave](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s20-behave.jpg)\n\n`s20-behave`\n\nHow you behave decides what a room is worth. If your policy is reckless and charges into firefights, a room is a death trap. If your policy takes the direct path to the exit, the exact same room is a gateway to freedom."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s21-grid","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s21-grid](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s21-grid.jpg)\n\n`s21-grid`\n\nLooking through the Architect's monitors, across a three-by-three grid of rooms, the top-left start evaluates to a high promise under a direct policy heading straight to the exit phone. But switch to random wandering, and that exact same room collapses to a fraction of its worth."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s22-evaluate","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s22-evaluate](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s22-evaluate.jpg)\n\n`s22-evaluate`\n\nBecause the Architect holds the transition model and reward map, he doesn't need to walk the hallways at all. He evaluates any policy directly at his desk using the Bellman expectation equation: taking the probability-weighted sum of each room's next steps."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-9","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s17-rules","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s17-rules](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s17-rules.jpg)\n\n`s17-rules`\n\nThis becomes particularly easy if you already know the game rules and know exactly what states will follow if you make a move.","x":-107,"y":-26},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s18-architect","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s18-architect](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s18-architect.jpg)\n\n`s18-architect`\n\nImagine you sit in the Architect's chair. You designed the Matrix. You hold the Keymaker's keyring: the complete model of the environment. You know where every door leads, and what reward sits behind every wall."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s19-desk","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s19-desk](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s19-desk.jpg)\n\n`s19-desk`\n\nWith that blueprint, you never need to set foot in a hallway. You can calculate what every room is worth right from your desk using Value Iteration."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-9","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s09-architect","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s09-architect](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s09-architect.jpg)\n\n`s09-architect` · 3:05\n\nNow take the Architect's chair. You built the Matrix. You hold the Keymaker's keys, a complete map of which door leads where. And in reinforcement learning, that map is called a model. With a model, you never have to walk the halls. You can compute every value from your chair, with an algorithm called value iteration."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-9","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 09 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-09","width":420,"height":306,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**09** · *The Architect* · `original`\n\nNow take the Architect's chair. You built the Matrix. You hold the Keymaker's keys: a complete map of which door leads where. In reinforcement learning, that map is called a model. With a model, you never have to walk the halls. You can compute every value from your chair, with an algorithm called value iteration.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Original: the Architect's wall of monitors, the keyring, the maze as a blueprint."}],"x":492,"y":-69},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-10","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s10-sweeps","width":400,"height":521,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s10-sweeps](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s10-sweeps.jpg)\n\n**10** · `4:01` · *Ch. 05: The Architect*\n\nTake three rooms in a row: A, then B, then C, where the phone waits, worth 100. Start by writing zero everywhere. Sweep one: C touches the phone, so C becomes 100. B looks at C's old value, zero, and stays zero. Sweep two: B sees C's 100, and becomes 0.9 times 100: 90. Sweep three: A sees B's 90, and becomes 81. Values ripple backward from the goal, one room per sweep, until nothing changes. The Architect solves the whole Matrix without taking a single step."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-10","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s23-sweeps","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s23-sweeps](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s23-sweeps.jpg)\n\n`s23-sweeps`\n\nAnd to discover the optimal path, he runs Value Iteration: sweeping Bellman updates across every room simultaneously. At the exit, the phone ignites first. On the next sweep, adjacent rooms absorb the discounted promise. Sweep by sweep, values ripple backward from the goal, exactly one step per pass."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s24-converge","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s24-converge](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s24-converge.jpg)\n\n`s24-converge`\n\nBecause the discount clock contracts the error on every sweep, the ripples settle and the numbers freeze into the optimal value, V*(s). When Neo stands at the entrance, he doesn't need to see the telephone four doors down; he simply steps toward whichever doorway shows the brightest glow. The Architect has solved the entire Matrix without taking a single physical step."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-10","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s20-sweeps","width":340,"height":487,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s20-sweeps](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s20-sweeps.jpg)\n\n`s20-sweeps`\n\nThe Architect starts with a guess of zero for every room. Then he sweeps across the entire grid, applying Bellman's equation at every doorway simultaneously. The exit lights up first. On the next sweep, neighbouring rooms absorb ninety percent of that promise. Pass by pass, values ripple backward from the goal until every number freezes. The Architect has solved the entire maze without taking a single physical step."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-10","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s10-sweeps","width":340,"height":509,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s10-sweeps](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s10-sweeps.jpg)\n\n`s10-sweeps` · 3:27\n\nTake three rooms in a row: A, then B, then C, where the phone waits, worth 100. Start by writing zero everywhere. Sweep one: C touches the phone, so C becomes 100. Sweep two: B sees C's 100, and becomes 0.9 times 100: 90. Sweep three: A sees B's 90, and becomes 81. Now zoom out. The same sweep runs across every room in the maze at once, and values ripple backward from the goal until nothing changes. The Architect solves the whole Matrix without taking a single step."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-10","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 10 (v3):\n\nPhone icon here too","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-10","width":420,"height":521,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**10** · *The Architect* · `original, then zoom out to the maze`\n\nTake three rooms in a row: A, then B, then C, we start from the phone, the final reward that worth 100. Start by writing zero everywhere else. Sweep one: C touches the phone, so C becomes 100. Sweep two: B sees C's 100, gets discounted by 0.9 and reads 90. Sweep three: A sees B's 90, and becomes 81. \n\nThe same sweep runs across every room in the maze at once, and values ripple backward from the goal until nothing changes.\n\n\nWhen Neo stands at the entrance, he doesn't need to see the telephone four doors down; he simply steps toward whichever doorway shows the biggest value. The Architect solves the whole Matrix without taking a single step.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Original's one-row A, B, C sweeps; the camera pulls back and the row becomes one corridor of the full maze 3d as in V1, where every room fills in.","x":0,"y":0}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-11","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s11-zion","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s11-zion](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s11-zion.jpg)\n\n**11** · `4:46` · *Ch. 06: Zion has no source code*\n\nBut value iteration needs the Architect's privilege: the source code. The rebels in Zion have none. When Neo jacks in, he has no idea whether door forty-two opens onto a rooftop or a furnace until he turns the handle. In the real world, from robots to language models, we almost never get the map. We have to learn from experience alone. There are two ways to do it."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-11","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s25-zion","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s25-zion](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s25-zion.jpg)\n\n`s25-zion`\n\nValue iteration is godlike, but it requires an Architect's privilege: complete access to the source code."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s26-jack","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s26-jack](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s26-jack.jpg)\n\n`s26-jack`\n\nThe rebels in Zion have no source code. When Neo jacks into the Matrix, he has no transition model. He cannot possibly know the grid by its entirety, he cannot know whether door forty-two opens onto a rooftop or drops into an incinerator until he turns the handle."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s27-modelfree","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s27-modelfree](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s27-modelfree.jpg)\n\n`s27-modelfree`\n\nIn the real world, from robotics to language models, we almost never get the blueprint. We are model-free. We only have trial, error, and raw experience. How do you learn what the rooms are worth when you must walk them in the dark?"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-11","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s21-zion","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s21-zion](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s21-zion.jpg)\n\n`s21-zion`\n\nValue iteration is godlike, but it requires an Architect's luxury: possessing the complete source code. The rebels in Zion have no source code. When Neo jacks into the city, he has no transition model. He cannot know whether door forty-two opens onto a rooftop or drops into an incinerator until he turns the handle."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s22-realworld","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s22-realworld](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s22-realworld.jpg)\n\n`s22-realworld`\n\nAnd that is the real world. A robot learning to walk on gravel has no formula for every shifting pebble. A language model has no equation predicting how a human will respond. We are model-free."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s23-dark","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s23-dark](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s23-dark.jpg)\n\n`s23-dark`\n\nWe cannot sit back and plan from an armchair. When you have no blueprint, you must learn from experience, testing doors one by one in the dark."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-11","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s11-zion","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s11-zion](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s11-zion.jpg)\n\n`s11-zion` · 4:07\n\nBut value iteration needs the Architect's privilege, the source code, and the rebels in Zion have none. When Neo jacks in, he has no idea whether door forty-two opens onto a rooftop or a furnace until he turns the handle. In the real world, from robots to language models, we almost never get the map. We have to learn from experience alone. There are two ways to do it."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-11","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 11 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-11","width":420,"height":284,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**11** · *Zion has no source code* · `original`\n\nBut value iteration needs the Architect's privilege: the source code. The rebels in Zion have none. When Neo jacks in, he has no idea whether door forty-two opens onto a rooftop or a furnace until he turns the handle. In the real world, from robots to language models, we almost never get the map. We have to learn from experience alone. There are two ways to do it.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Original."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-12","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s12-sixth","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s12-sixth](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s12-sixth.jpg)\n\n**12** · `5:20` · *Ch. 07: You are the sixth*\n\nThe first way is written into the film itself. The Architect tells Neo that he is the sixth. Five Ones came before him, and each walked the path all the way to its end. That is Monte Carlo learning. No map, no guessing. You play a whole run, from start to finish, and only then add up what you got."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-12","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s28-sixth","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s28-sixth](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s28-sixth.jpg)\n\n`s28-sixth`\n\nThe Architect once told Neo: \"The Matrix is older than you know. You are the sixth.\" Before him, five predecessors had walked the anomaly's path. That is the first way to learn without a model: Monte Carlo learning."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s29-branches","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s29-branches](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s29-branches.jpg)\n\n`s29-branches`\n\nLook at how the world branches before you. From where Neo stands in Room A, every choice splits into new doors, ambushes, and dead ends. When you have no transition map, you cannot calculate probabilities for every branch. So how does Monte Carlo approximate the true value V(A)?","x":-142,"y":-111},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s30-dice","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s30-dice](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s30-dice.jpg)\n\n`s30-dice`\n\nIt rolls the dice. It refuses to guess or approximate mid-run. You send an agent on an entire run from Room A all the way to terminal victory or death. When the run ends, you tally the full accumulated return, G."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-12","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s24-sixth","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s24-sixth](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s24-sixth.jpg)\n\n`s24-sixth`\n\nNeo has been doing exactly that, but painfully. When the Architect meets Neo, he reveals a chilling truth: \"The Matrix is older than you know. You are the sixth.\" Five predecessors walked this path before him. That history shows the simplest way to learn without a model: Monte Carlo learning."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s25-rule","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s25-rule](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s25-rule.jpg)\n\n`s25-rule`\n\nMonte Carlo follows one strict rule: never guess mid-run. You send an agent through the maze and let the attempt play out completely, until they either reach the phone or fail. Only when the run finishes do you calculate the full accumulated return."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-12","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s12-sixth","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s12-sixth](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s12-sixth.jpg)\n\n`s12-sixth` · 4:35\n\nThe first way is written right into the film. The Architect tells Neo that he is the sixth. Five Ones came before him, and each walked the path all the way to its end. That is Monte Carlo learning. No map, no guessing. You play a whole run, from start to finish, and only then add up what you got."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-12","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 12 (v3):\n\nI want the fancy 3d tree in v1","fill":"#ffe066","x":0,"y":0},{"type":"note","id":"new-12","width":420,"height":396,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**12** · *You are the sixth* · `original, v1's tree`\n\nThe first way is written into the film itself. The Architect tells Neo that he is the sixth. Five Ones came before him, and each walked the path all the way to its end. That is Monte Carlo learning.\n\nLook at how the world branches before you. From where Neo stands in Room A, every choice splits into new doors, ambushes, and dead ends. When you have no transition map, you cannot calculate probabilities for every branch. So how does Monte Carlo approximate the true value V(A)?\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Opens on the original; then v1's branching tree from room A, with one full run tracing a branch to its end."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-13","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s13-ledger","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s13-ledger](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s13-ledger.jpg)\n\n**13** · `5:49` · *Ch. 07: You are the sixth*\n\nLook at the ledger for room A. The first One went left and was ambushed: zero. The second fell through a trapdoor: zero. The third found the phone in three steps: 81. The fourth took a longer route: 72.9. The fifth was captured at once: zero. Monte Carlo's estimate for room A is simply the average: about 31. With more runs, that average closes in on the true value."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-13","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s31-ledger","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s31-ledger](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s31-ledger.jpg)\n\n`s31-ledger`\n\nLook at the operator's ledger across the five previous Ones. Some took bad doors, walked into ambushes, and died empty-handed. Others climbed service ladders or discovered detours, reaching ringing hardlines with high payouts."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-13","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s26-ledger","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s26-ledger](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s26-ledger.jpg)\n\n`s26-ledger`\n\nLook at the operator's ledger across the five previous Ones. Some took bad doors, hit traps, and scored zero. Others found clean paths to the exit and collected the reward. Monte Carlo simply averages those completed runs to score each room."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-13","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s13-ledger","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s13-ledger](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s13-ledger.jpg)\n\n`s13-ledger` · 4:56\n\nLook at the ledger for room A. The first One went left and was ambushed: zero. The second fell through a trapdoor: zero. The third found the phone in three steps: 81. The fourth took a longer route: 72.9. The fifth was captured at once: zero. Monte Carlo's estimate for room A is simply the average: about 31. With more runs, that average closes in on the true value."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-13","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 13 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-13","width":420,"height":350,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**13** · *You are the sixth* · `original narration, v1 tree + original table`\n\nLook at the ledger for room A. The first One went left and was ambushed: zero. The second fell through a trapdoor: zero. The third found the phone in three steps and gets a discounted reward of 81. The fourth took a longer route: 72.9. The fifth was captured at once: zero. Monte Carlo's estimate for room A is simply the average: about 31. With more runs, that average closes in on the true value.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v1's tree on the right, one branch per One; the original's ledger table on the left fills row by row, then the average 31 is computed underneath."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-14","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s14-mccost","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s14-mccost](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s14-mccost.jpg)\n\n**14** · `6:23` · *Ch. 07: You are the sixth*\n\nIt needs no model, and it is honest: it never trusts a guess. But it is noisy, and it is slow. If Neo makes a fatal mistake at the second door, Monte Carlo learns nothing until the whole run is over. Waiting for the end of the world to learn your first lesson is an expensive way to train."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-14","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s32-variance","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s32-variance](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s32-variance.jpg)\n\n`s32-variance`\n\nIt is completely model-free and completely unbiased. But look at the terrible cost: high variance, and zero learning during the episode. Five predecessors are far from enough; in an exponential maze of shifting traps, a handful of runs barely scratches the surface. You would need hundreds or thousands of full runs before that noisy average truly settles."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s33-wait","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s33-wait](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s33-wait.jpg)\n\n`s33-wait`\n\nWorse, if Neo makes a fatal mistake at door two, Monte Carlo forces him to suffer through thirty more rooms and get blasted before updating a single number. Waiting for the end of the world to learn your first lesson is a brutal way to train."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-14","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s27-cost","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s27-cost](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s27-cost.jpg)\n\n`s27-cost`\n\nIt is completely unbiased, but look at the cost: if Neo makes a fatal mistake at door two, Monte Carlo makes him suffer through thirty more rooms before updating a single number. Waiting for the end of the world to learn your first lesson is a brutal way to train."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-14","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s14-cost","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s14-cost](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s14-cost.jpg)\n\n`s14-cost` · 5:23\n\nIt is completely unbiased, but look at the cost: if Neo makes a fatal mistake at door two, Monte Carlo makes him suffer through thirty more rooms before updating a single number. Waiting for the end of the world to learn your first lesson is a brutal way to train."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-14","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 14 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-14","width":420,"height":284,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**14** · *You are the sixth* · `v2, with original's drop-stone`\n\nIt is completely unbiased, but look at the cost: if Neo makes a fatal mistake at door two, Monte Carlo makes him suffer through thirty more rooms before updating a single number. Waiting for the end of the world to learn your first lesson is a brutal way to train.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v2's visuals; on \"brutal way to train\" the original's heavy stone drops and slams the frame."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-15","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s15-bullet","width":400,"height":433,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s15-bullet](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s15-bullet.jpg)\n\n**15** · `6:48` · *Ch. 08: Bullet time*\n\nThe second way asks a simple question: why wait? In 1988, Richard Sutton described temporal difference learning, or TD, which updates after a single step. Think of it as bullet time. The world freezes between one room and the next, just long enough to learn."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-15","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s34-td","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s34-td](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s34-td.jpg)\n\n`s34-td`\n\nMonte Carlo had a serious weakness: to learn what a single rooftop was worth, Neo had to survive, or die, and wait for the entire run to finish. But what if he could learn after just one step? This is temporal-difference learning, or TD."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-15","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s28-td","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s28-td](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s28-td.jpg)\n\n`s28-td`\n\nMonte Carlo had a glaring weakness. But what if you could learn after just one step? This is temporal-difference learning, or TD. Instead of waiting for the end of the story, you learn while the journey is still happening."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-15","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s15-td","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s15-td](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s15-td.jpg)\n\n`s15-td` · 5:45\n\nMonte Carlo had a glaring weakness. But what if you could learn after just one step? This is temporal-difference learning, or TD. Instead of waiting for the end of the story, you learn while the journey is still happening."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-15","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 15 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-15","width":420,"height":240,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**15** · *Learn every step* · `v2`\n\nMonte Carlo does have a glaring weakness. But what if you could learn after just one step? This is temporal-difference learning, or TD. Instead of waiting for the end of the story, you learn while the journey is still happening.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v2."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-16","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s16-tderror","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s16-tderror](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s16-tderror.jpg)\n\n**16** · `7:12` · *Ch. 08: Bullet time*\n\nNeo is in hallway twelve. He believes it is worth 5. He steps into room fourteen. The reward: zero. But he already has a guess for room fourteen: 7. So by Bellman's logic, the hallway should have been worth zero, plus 0.9 times 7: 6.3. His old belief was 5. The gap, 1.3, is the TD error. It measures surprise."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-16","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s35-dodge","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s35-dodge](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s35-dodge.jpg)\n\n`s35-dodge`\n\nNeo dodges one bullet and lands in a new state. He doesn't know how his story will end, and he doesn't wait to find out. Instead, he looks at what he already believes the next state is worth. With an estimate of sixty, zero immediate reward, and a discount of point nine, TD constructs a fresh target: fifty-four."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s36-error","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s36-error](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s36-error.jpg)\n\n`s36-error`\n\nComparing that new target with his old belief yields the temporal-difference error. If the step went better than expected, the value rises; if worse, it drops. Dodge. Observe. Update. Neo is learning while the future is still happening."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-16","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s29-ab","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s29-ab](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s29-ab.jpg)\n\n`s29-ab`\n\nYou stand in room A, take a step into room B, and note any immediate reward. Then, without waiting to see how the run ends, you look at your current guess for room B. You update room A's score toward that reward plus gamma times your guess for room B. If the step went better than expected, the score goes up; if worse, it drops."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-16","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s16-tderror","width":340,"height":553,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s16-tderror](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s16-tderror.jpg)\n\n`s16-tderror` · 6:00\n\nYou stand in room A, believing it is worth 40. You step into room B and collect a reward of 5. Without waiting to see how the run ends, you look at your current guess for room B: 50. So room A should have been worth 5, plus 0.9 times 50, which comes to 50. The gap between 50 and your old 40 is plus 10: the TD error. You move room A's score halfway toward the target: 45. Next time, the step goes worse: a penalty of minus 20. The target falls to 25, the error is 25 minus 45, minus 20, and room A drops from 45 to 35. This is bootstrapping: updating a prediction using another prediction."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-16","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 16 (v3):\n\nunclear how the calculation bottom left connects to the rooms, make it more integrated","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-16","width":420,"height":460,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**16** · *Learn every step* · `v2, with its numbers`\n\nYou stand in room A, believing it is worth 40. You step into room B and collect a reward of 5. Without waiting to see how the run ends, you look at your current guess for room B: 50. So room A should have been worth 5, plus 0.9 times 50, which comes to 50. The gap between 50 and your old 40 is plus 10: the TD error. You move room A's score halfway toward the target: 45. Next time, the step goes worse: a penalty of minus 20. The target falls to 25, the error is 25 minus 45, minus 20, and room A drops from 45 to 35. This is bootstrapping: updating a prediction using another prediction.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v2's A-to-B 3D rooms and HUD, with every number written as it is spoken: V(A) 40, r 5, V(B) 50, target 5 + 0.9 × 50 = 50, error +10, V(A) counts up to 45 in amber; then r −20, target 25, error −20, V(A) counts down to 35 in red."}],"x":-250,"y":-31},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-17","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s17-bootstrap","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s17-bootstrap](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s17-bootstrap.jpg)\n\n**17** · `7:44` · *Ch. 08: Bullet time*\n\nHe does not jump all the way to 6.3, because his guess about room fourteen might be wrong too. He moves halfway: 5.65. Then time snaps back, and he keeps running. This is called bootstrapping: correcting one guess with a slightly better one. It turns learning from a post-mortem into instinct."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-17","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s37-bootstrap","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s37-bootstrap](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s37-bootstrap.jpg)\n\n`s37-bootstrap`\n\nThis is bootstrapping: updating a prediction using another prediction. It trades a little initial bias for much lower variance and immediate online learning."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s38-bigpicture","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s38-bigpicture](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s38-bigpicture.jpg)\n\n`s38-bigpicture`\n\nAnd here lies the big picture. The Architect's value iteration computed the full mathematical expectation across every possible future. Temporal difference samples just one real experienced transition at a time. That single shift is what lets reinforcement learning leave the Architect's desk and survive in the real world."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-17","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s30-bootstrap","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s30-bootstrap](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s30-bootstrap.jpg)\n\n`s30-bootstrap`\n\nThis is bootstrapping: updating a prediction using another prediction. It trades a little initial bias for much lower noise and instant feedback. While the Architect computed every possible future in his head, temporal difference learns values from one real step at a time."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-17","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"v3-none-17","width":340,"content":"*Dropped in v3.*","fill":"#e9ecef"}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-17","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 17 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-17","width":420,"fill":"#e9ecef","content":"**17** · ~~dropped~~\n\n(Bootstrapping merged into frame 16.)"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-18","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s18-dejavu","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s18-dejavu](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s18-dejavu.jpg)\n\n**18** · `8:12` · *Ch. 09: Déjà vu*\n\nThen a black cat walks past. And then the same cat, again. Déjà vu: a glitch in the Matrix. It happens when they change something. A wall appears where a door used to be. The Architect's map is now wrong, and every value computed from it is stale. But a TD learner does not care. It feels the surprise, and rewrites its numbers as it goes."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-18","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"v1-18-none","width":340,"content":"*No matching take in this version.*","fill":"#e9ecef"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-18","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"v2-18-none","width":340,"content":"*No matching take in this version.*","fill":"#e9ecef"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-18","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"v3-none-18","width":340,"content":"*Dropped in v3.*","fill":"#e9ecef"}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-18","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 18 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-18","width":420,"fill":"#e9ecef","content":"**18** · ~~dropped~~\n\n(The déjà vu scene is dropped.)"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-19","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s19-qlearning","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s19-qlearning](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s19-qlearning.jpg)\n\n**19** · `8:45` · *Ch. 09: Déjà vu*\n\nThere is one more problem. A value tells Neo how good a hallway is, not which door to open. So he learns a number for each door instead: an action value, called Q. Left is 4, centre is 6, right is 2. He takes the centre. Behind it, the best door ahead is worth 9. So the new target is 0.9 times 9: 8.1. Centre moves from 6 halfway toward 8.1: 7.05. That is Q-learning."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-19","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s39-catch","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s39-catch](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s39-catch.jpg)\n\n`s39-catch`\n\nHere is the catch with everything we've calculated so far. We know how to estimate the value of a room, V(s). But remember: we don't have the Architect's model. We have no map."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s40-threedoors","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s40-threedoors](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s40-threedoors.jpg)\n\n`s40-threedoors`\n\nSo when Neo stands in this corridor facing three closed doors, knowing the current state is worth fifty or eighty is completely useless. He doesn't know which room door left opens into, or what trap door right hides. Without a transition model, a room's value cannot tell you which door to choose."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s41-q","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s41-q](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s41-q.jpg)\n\n`s41-q`\n\nTo navigate in the dark, you cannot just score the room. You must evaluate each door. In reinforcement learning, that is the Action-Value function: Q(s, a), the quality of taking a specific action a in state s."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s42-qtable","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s42-qtable](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s42-qtable.jpg)\n\n`s42-qtable`\n\nInstead of glowing tiles on the floor, imagine numbers painted directly onto the handles. This lookup grid of every room and every door is called a Q-table. Neo doesn't need transition blueprints or probabilities. He simply checks his Q-table and turns the door handle with the highest score."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-19","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s31-threedoors","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s31-threedoors](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s31-threedoors.jpg)\n\n`s31-threedoors`\n\nThere is still one critical puzzle left. Suppose you know the exact value of every room in the building. You stand in a hallway facing three closed doors. Which one do you open? Knowing room values does not help you, because without a map, you don't know which door leads where! A high-value room is useless if you don't know which doorway reaches it."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s32-q","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s32-q](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s32-q.jpg)\n\n`s32-q`\n\nThe solution is ingenious: stop scoring the rooms, and start scoring the doors. In reinforcement learning, that is the Action-Value function, or Q(s, a). Instead of glowing numbers on the floor, imagine scores stamped directly onto each door handle. You don't need a map or transition probabilities. You simply look at the handles and turn the one with the highest score."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-19","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s17-doors","width":340,"height":487,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s17-doors](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s17-doors.jpg)\n\n`s17-doors` · 6:49\n\nThere is one more problem. A room's value tells you how good it is to stand there, not which door to open, and without a map you don't know where each door leads. So Neo learns a number for each door instead: an action value, called Q. Left is 4, centre is 6, right is 2. He takes the centre. Behind it, the best door ahead is worth 9. So the new target is 0.9 times 9: 8.1. Centre moves from 6 halfway toward 8.1: 7.05. That is Q-learning."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-19","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 19 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-19","width":420,"height":428,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**19** · *Score the doors* · `original numbers, v2 s03 3D`\n\nThere is one more problem. A room's value tells you how good it is to stand there, not which door to open, and without a map you don't know where each door leads. So Neo learns a number for each door instead: an action value, called Q. In our case, left is 4, centre is 6, right is 2. He takes the centre. Behind it, the best door ahead is worth 9. So the new target is 0.9 times 9: 8.1. We use the same temporal difference technique to go back and update the scores of the action we just picked--going Centre from the previous room moves from 6 halfway toward 8.1, leaving it at 7.05. That is Q-learning.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** Reuse v2 s03's 3D corridors: Neo facing three doors, with a score stamped on each handle; the centre door opens onto the next room's doors, and the centre score counts up from 6 to 7.05."}],"x":774,"y":-31},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-20","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s20-atari","width":400,"height":433,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s20-atari](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s20-atari.jpg)\n\n**20** · `9:26` · *Ch. 09: Déjà vu*\n\nIn 2013, researchers at DeepMind replaced Neo's table of numbers with a neural network, and pointed it at Atari games. The pixels were the state. The joystick was the action. It learned to play Breakout from nothing but the score."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-20","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s43-dqn","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s43-dqn](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s43-dqn.jpg)\n\n`s43-dqn`\n\nBy 2015, DeepMind had taken this exact equation, replaced Neo's Q-table with a deep neural network, and unleashed it on 49 Atari games. The screen pixels were the state, the joystick buttons were the actions, and Deep Q-Networks learned to play Breakout and Space Invaders directly from experience, outscoring expert human players on many of them."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-20","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s33-dqn","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s33-dqn](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s33-dqn.jpg)\n\n`s33-dqn`\n\nIn 2013, DeepMind took this exact equation, connected it to a deep neural network, and unleashed it on Atari games. Given only raw screen pixels as states and joystick buttons as actions, Deep Q-Networks learned to play Breakout and Space Invaders at superhuman levels, purely from trial and error."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-20","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s18-dqn","width":340,"height":443,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s18-dqn](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s18-dqn.jpg)\n\n`s18-dqn` · 7:25\n\nIn 2013, DeepMind took this exact equation, connected it to a deep neural network, and unleashed it on Atari games. It saw only raw screen pixels as its state, and pushed joystick buttons as its actions. And these Deep Q-Networks learned to play Breakout and Space Invaders at superhuman levels, purely from trial and error."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-20","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 20 (v3):\n\nLet's use v1's instead, the wireframe one looks a little boring","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-20","width":420,"height":262,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**20** · *Score the doors* · `v2`\n\nIn 2013, DeepMind took this exact equation, connected it to a deep neural network, and unleashed it on Atari games. Given only raw screen pixels as states and joystick buttons as actions, Deep Q-Networks learned to play Breakout and Space Invaders at superhuman levels, purely from trial and error.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v2's SVG."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-21","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s21-spoon","width":400,"height":477,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s21-spoon](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s21-spoon.jpg)\n\n**21** · `9:47` · *Ch. 10: There is no spoon*\n\nThere is no spoon. All of this comes down to two questions. Do you have the source code? And how far do you look before you update? With the map, one step at a time, you are the Architect: value iteration. With no map, waiting for the end, you are the five Ones before: Monte Carlo. With no map, learning at every door, you are Neo: temporal difference."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-21","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s44-twoq","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s44-twoq](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s44-twoq.jpg)\n\n`s44-twoq`\n\nThe entire landscape of reinforcement learning is not an untamable forest of math. It boils down to two simple questions: Do you possess the source code? And how far do you look before updating your mind?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s45-taxonomy","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s45-taxonomy](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s45-taxonomy.jpg)\n\n`s45-taxonomy`\n\nIf you have the model and step once, you are the Architect using Value Iteration. If you have no model and wait until the end, you are the five previous Ones using Monte Carlo. And if you have no model and update on every single door, you are Neo using Temporal Difference."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-21","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s34-twoq","width":340,"height":377,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s34-twoq](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s34-twoq.jpg)\n\n`s34-twoq`\n\nWhen you zoom out, the entire landscape of reinforcement learning is not an untamable forest of math. It boils down to two simple questions: Do you have a model of your world? And how far do you look before updating your mind?"},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s35-taxonomy","width":340,"height":421,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s35-taxonomy](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s35-taxonomy.jpg)\n\n`s35-taxonomy`\n\nIf you have the model and calculate one step ahead, you are the Architect using Value Iteration. If you have no model and wait until the run finishes, you are averaging the five Ones with Monte Carlo. And if you have no model and learn door by door, you are using Temporal Difference and Q-learning."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-21","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s19-twoq","width":340,"height":531,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s19-twoq](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s19-twoq.jpg)\n\n`s19-twoq` · 7:50\n\nZoom out, and the entire landscape of reinforcement learning is not an untamable forest of math. It boils down to two simple questions: Do you have a model of your world? And how far do you look before updating your mind? If you have the model and calculate one step ahead, you are the Architect using value iteration. If you have no model and wait until the run finishes, you are averaging the five Ones with Monte Carlo. And if you have no model and learn door by door, you are using temporal difference and Q-learning."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-21","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 21 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-21","width":420,"height":394,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**21** · *We are all learning* · `v2 narration, v1's 2×2`\n\nWhen you zoom out, the entire landscape of reinforcement learning is not an untamable forest of math. It boils down to two simple questions: Do you have a model of your world? And how far do you look before updating your mind? If you have the model and calculate one step ahead, you are the Architect using value iteration. If you have no model and wait until the run finishes, you are averaging the five Ones with Monte Carlo. And if you have no model and learn door by door, you are using temporal difference and Q-learning.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v1's two-by-two comparison grid: model or no model across, one step or whole run down, each cell filling as it is named."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-row-22","layout":"horizontal","gap":48,"alignItems":"start","children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-neo-s22-agent","width":400,"height":455,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s22-agent](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/script/s22-agent.jpg)\n\n**22** · `10:21` · *Ch. 10: There is no spoon*\n\nFor more than twenty-five years, we were told the men in sunglasses were the Agents. But in reinforcement learning, an agent is not the one who guards the system. It is the one who enters an uncertain world, makes mistakes, and learns its way to the exit. Neo was the agent all along."},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v1-22","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s46-cinema","width":340,"height":333,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s46-cinema](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s46-cinema.jpg)\n\n`s46-cinema`\n\nFor twenty-five years, cinema framed this story as a war against villains in sunglasses. But look at what the math reveals."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s47-learning","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s47-learning](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s47-learning.jpg)\n\n`s47-learning`\n\nThe simulation was never a static cage. The Matrix is learning, and we are all learning in it. Every time you make a decision in the dark, absorb a surprise, and adjust your expectation for tomorrow, you run the very same Bellman equation."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v1-s48-end","width":340,"height":289,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s48-end](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v1-s48-end.jpg)\n\n`s48-end`\n\nWe are all but a learning simulation."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v2-22","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s36-cinema","width":340,"height":355,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s36-cinema](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s36-cinema.jpg)\n\n`s36-cinema`\n\nFor twenty-five years, cinema framed this story as an action movie about heroes and villains. But look at what the mathematics actually tells us."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s37-learning","width":340,"height":399,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s37-learning](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s37-learning.jpg)\n\n`s37-learning`\n\nThe simulation was never a static prison. The Matrix is learning, and we are all learning in it. Every time you make a decision in the dark, observe the outcome, and update your expectations for tomorrow, you run that very same Bellman equation."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v2-s38-end","width":340,"height":311,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s38-end](../../assets/neo-was-the-agent/compare/v2-s38-end.jpg)\n\n`s38-end`\n\nBelieve it or not, we are all but a learning simulation."}]},{"type":"frame","id":"cmp-v3-22","layout":"vertical","gap":16,"width":340,"children":[{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s20-cinema","width":340,"height":465,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s20-cinema](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s20-cinema.jpg)\n\n`s20-cinema` · 8:23\n\nFor twenty-five years, cinema framed this story as a war against villains in sunglasses. But look at what the math reveals. The simulation was never a static prison. The Matrix is learning, and we are all learning in it. Every time you make a decision in the dark, observe the outcome, and update your expectations for tomorrow, you run that very same Bellman equation."},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-v3-s21-end","width":340,"height":311,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"![s21-end](../../assets/learning-simulation-v3/scenes/s21-end.jpg)\n\n`s21-end` · 8:48\n\nBelieve it or not, we are all but a learning simulation."}]},{"type":"note","id":"cmp-fb-22","width":280,"content":"Feedback on 22 (v3):","fill":"#ffe066"},{"type":"note","id":"new-22","width":420,"height":328,"metadata":{"type":"decks.markdown"},"content":"**22** · *We are all learning* · `v1 s46, then v2 s37 and s38`\n\nFor twenty-five years, cinema framed this story as a war against villains in sunglasses. But look at what the math reveals. Every time you make a decision in the dark, observe the outcome, and update your expectations for tomorrow, you run that very same Bellman equation. The same goes for LLMs, robots, and maybe even ourselves. Some might still say, we are all part of a learning simulation.\n\n---\n\n**On screen:** v1 s46's visuals, then v2 s37 and s38 to the end."}]}],"x":886,"y":45769},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-1","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s01-wake","to":"cmp-neo-s02-credit"},"x":1070,"y":46176,"width":32,"height":806,"viewBox":[0,0,32,806],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 802 M4 782 L16 802 L28 782","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-2","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s02-credit","to":"cmp-neo-s03-loop"},"x":1070,"y":47429,"width":32,"height":391,"viewBox":[0,0,32,391],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 387 M4 367 L16 387 L28 367","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-3","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s03-loop","to":"cmp-neo-s04-agent"},"x":1070,"y":48245,"width":32,"height":413,"viewBox":[0,0,32,413],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 409 M4 389 L16 409 L28 389","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-4","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s04-agent","to":"cmp-neo-s05-oracle"},"x":1070,"y":49105,"width":32,"height":872,"viewBox":[0,0,32,872],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 868 M4 848 L16 868 L28 848","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-5","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s05-oracle","to":"cmp-neo-s06-value"},"x":1070,"y":50380,"width":32,"height":98,"viewBox":[0,0,32,98],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 94 M4 74 L16 94 L28 74","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-6","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s06-value","to":"cmp-neo-s07-bellman"},"x":1070,"y":50947,"width":32,"height":806,"viewBox":[0,0,32,806],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 802 M4 782 L16 802 L28 782","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-7","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s07-bellman","to":"cmp-neo-s08-discount"},"x":1070,"y":52178,"width":32,"height":696,"viewBox":[0,0,32,696],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 692 M4 672 L16 692 L28 672","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-8","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s08-discount","to":"cmp-neo-s09-architect"},"x":1070,"y":53343,"width":32,"height":413,"viewBox":[0,0,32,413],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 409 M4 389 L16 409 L28 389","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-9","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s09-architect","to":"cmp-neo-s10-sweeps"},"x":1070,"y":54203,"width":32,"height":1680,"viewBox":[0,0,32,1680],"geometry":"M16 4 L16 1676 M4 1656 L16 1676 L28 1656","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"},{"type":"path","id":"cmp-arrow-10","stroke":"#2f9e44","strokeWidth":4,"metadata":{"type":"decks.arrow","from":"cmp-neo-s10-sweeps","to":"cmp-neo-s11-zion"},"x":1070,"y":56396,"widt